How do the mTOR inhibitors work?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
How do the mTOR inhibitors work?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","mTOR inhibitors are a class of drugs that target the mechanistic cell cell kinase (mTOR) enzyme. mTOR is a serine-to-serreonine kinase that plays a crucial role in cell growth, growth, and survival. It is a key component of two signaling pathways: mTOR-1 and mTOR-2. These pathways are involved in processes such as cell growth, proliferation, differentiation, and stress response.

mTOR inhibitors are used in cancer treatment and other diseases where mTOR signaling is over-regulated. These inhibitors work by binding to and inhibiting the activity of the mTOR enzyme, thereby inhibiting the activity of the two signaling pathways.

mTOR inhibitors are classified into two main classes: inhibitors of mTOR-1 and inhibitors that inhibit both mTOR-1 and mTOR-2. The primary drugs used in cancer treatment are dual inhibitors, targeting both isoforms.",relevance,"# Task Description
