### Step 1: Understand the structure
Cyclamate is a disester of sodium or potassium salicylate. This means it consists of two ester groups attached to the carboxylate group of salicylic acid, with sodium or potassium ions as the counterions.

### Step 2: Identify the parent structure
The parent structure is **salicylic acid**, which is **2,4-dihydroxybenzoic acid**.

### Step 3: Identify the ester group
The ester group is derived from **glycol** (ethylene glycol, CH₂OH-OH).

### Step 4: Combine the parts
The two glycol groups react with the two carboxylate groups of salicylic acid to form a diesster. The structure is:

\[
\text{HOOC-C₆H₃-CH₃\text{OH}} \xrightarrow{\text{Glycol}} \text{HOOC-C₆H₃-CH₂\text{O}CH₂\text{O}}
\]

### Step 4: Naming the compound
The general name for cyclamate is **sodium cyclamate** or **potassium cyclamate**, depending on the counterion. The IUPAC name for sodium cyclamate is:

\[
\boxed{\text{Na}_2\text{C}_8\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_4\text{O}_2\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{O}}
\]

