<USER_REQUEST>
An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The forward amplifier gain is \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback gain is \( m_v = 0.1 \). The closed-loop voltage gain is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
A_{fv} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V}
\]
The amplifier's gain decreases by 6 dB due to aging. The new forward gain \( A'_v \) is:
\[
A'_v = \frac{A_v}{10} = \frac{100}{10} = 10
\]
The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A'_v \) is:
\[
A'_f = \frac{A'_v}{1 - m_y} = \frac{10}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{10}{0.9} \approx 11.11 \text{ V}
\]
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A'_f - A_{fv}}{A_{fv}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{11.11 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-100}{111.11} \right) \times 100 \approx -89.99\%
\]
Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-89.99\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

