The Coriolis effect is particularly significant in large-scale ocean currents due to their large flow rates and large distances they travel. The Coriolis effect deflects these currents to the right in the right side of the Earth and to the left in the left side of the Earth. This deflection is responsible for the formation of meandering currents and the formation of gybles in the ocean.

